        <mxCell id="band51" value="THE ACL BOUNDARY   ·   cloud-container/mosquitto/aclfile" style="rounded=1;arcSize=3;fillColor=#FFF7F7;strokeColor=#C62828;strokeWidth=2;dashed=1;dashPattern=8 5;html=1;verticalAlign=top;align=left;spacingLeft=16;spacingTop=10;fontSize=13;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#C62828;shadow=0;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="660" width="810" height="250" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="c52" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;line-height:1.45&quot;&gt;&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px;color:#16202B;&quot;&gt;pattern rules for every robot&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:10px;color:#5A6B7B&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;pattern write robots/&lt;b&gt;%u&lt;/b&gt;/telemetry&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;code&gt;pattern write robots/&lt;b&gt;%u&lt;/b&gt;/status&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;code&gt;pattern read &amp;nbsp;robots/&lt;b&gt;%u&lt;/b&gt;/cmd&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;code&gt;%u&lt;/code&gt; is the authenticated username. A robot&#39;s username &lt;i&gt;is&lt;/i&gt; its &lt;code&gt;robot_id&lt;/code&gt;, so one rule block scopes every robot to its own namespace. Onboarding robot #2 is a new credential, never an edit here.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=10;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFEBEE;strokeColor=#C62828;strokeWidth=2;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;spacingRight=8;shadow=0;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="70" y="706" width="360" height="176" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="c53" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;line-height:1.45&quot;&gt;&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px;color:#16202B;&quot;&gt;explicit rules for the one backend&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:10px;color:#5A6B7B&quot;&gt;It needs fleet-wide visibility (&lt;code&gt;robots/+/…&lt;/code&gt;), so it gets fixed rules, not a pattern.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;read&lt;/b&gt; on telemetry / health / status / heartbeat / lidar / camera-answer&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;write&lt;/b&gt; on cmd and camera/offer&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The asymmetry is the point.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=10;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFEBEE;strokeColor=#C62828;strokeWidth=2;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;spacingRight=8;shadow=0;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="452" y="706" width="368" height="176" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="nt54" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;line-height:1.5;font-size:11px;color:#16202B&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why the backend gets &lt;code&gt;read&lt;/code&gt;, never &lt;code&gt;readwrite&lt;/code&gt;, on telemetry.&lt;/b&gt; The backend publishing there would mean impersonating a robot&#39;s own self-reported state — a compromised backend could lie about a battery level. The only things it is trusted to &lt;i&gt;originate&lt;/i&gt; are a command and an SDP offer, both of which it initiates on an operator&#39;s behalf.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why an SDP offer is not an exception to that.&lt;/b&gt; An offer is not a robot self-report. It is the same category &lt;code&gt;cmd&lt;/code&gt; was already in — an instance of an existing rule, not a new hole. The &lt;code&gt;aclfile&lt;/code&gt;&#39;s own comments say so.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;code&gt;test_backend_cannot_publish_robot_telemetry&lt;/code&gt; exists to keep this true as the codebase grows.&lt;/div&gt;" style="shape=note;size=16;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFF7F7;strokeColor=#C62828;strokeWidth=2;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=8;spacingTop=4;spacingRight=6;shadow=0;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="882" y="660" width="798" height="250" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="nt55" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;line-height:1.5;font-size:11px;color:#16202B&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Last Will and Testament — the single most important reliability pattern in this contract.&lt;/b&gt; A retained &lt;code&gt;status&lt;/code&gt; tells you a robot &lt;i&gt;is&lt;/i&gt; up. It cannot tell you a robot went down ungracefully — a crash, a pulled cable, &lt;code&gt;docker kill&lt;/code&gt; — because no code is left running to publish &#39;offline&#39;. So the client registers a Will at connect time and the &lt;b&gt;broker itself&lt;/b&gt; publishes it when the TCP connection dies. Clean shutdown → the agent publishes offline. Unclean death → the broker does. Either way &lt;code&gt;robots/{id}/status&lt;/code&gt; always reflects reality, retained, immediately.&lt;/div&gt;" style="shape=note;size=16;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFF8E1;strokeColor=#F9A825;strokeWidth=2;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=8;spacingTop=4;spacingRight=6;shadow=0;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="940" width="1640" height="100" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l56" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;line-height:1.5;font-size:10px;color:#90A4AE&quot;&gt;docs/03-mqtt-layer.md · docs/api-reference.md · Credentials are generated at container start from env vars — nothing secret is ever committed&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=0;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1034" width="1640" height="24" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
