        <mxCell id="e55" value="DTLS-SRTP media" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=1;curved=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;strokeColor=#2E7D32;strokeWidth=4;dashed=0;endArrow=blockThin;endFill=1;endSize=6;fontSize=10;fontColor=#2E7D32;labelBackgroundColor=#FFFFFF;spacingTop=-2;exitX=0.5;exitY=0;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0.5;entryY=1;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="p47" target="p37">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="nt56" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;line-height:1.5;font-size:11px;color:#16202B&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why the robot does &lt;i&gt;not&lt;/i&gt; move into AWS.&lt;/b&gt; This is the original thesis restated concretely: &lt;code&gt;robot-container&lt;/code&gt; runs on or next to the robot today (Docker on a laptop) and tomorrow (a Jetson bolted to a physical Turtlebot3) — never in AWS. ROS2/DDS needs a flat, low-latency local network. Migrating &#39;to AWS&#39; means the &lt;i&gt;cloud&lt;/i&gt; half moves; the robot half was always going to live at the edge.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why not Kinesis Video Streams WebRTC?&lt;/b&gt; Swapping to its signalling model would &lt;i&gt;replace&lt;/i&gt; what Milestones 8–9 built and verified, not migrate it. coturn on EC2 is the direct, no-redesign equivalent — same config, same turn:// URL shape, a public IP instead of a container.&lt;/div&gt;" style="shape=note;size=16;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFF8E1;strokeColor=#F9A825;strokeWidth=2;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=8;spacingTop=4;spacingRight=6;shadow=0;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="898" y="660" width="782" height="200" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="nt57" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;line-height:1.5;font-size:11px;color:#16202B&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;What was actually verified, since nothing was provisioned.&lt;/b&gt; The &#39;config over hardcoding&#39; claim this entire guide depends on was &lt;i&gt;audited, not assumed&lt;/i&gt;: grepping every .py file in &lt;code&gt;robot_agent/&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;backend/app/&lt;/code&gt; for &lt;code&gt;mosquitto&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;localhost&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;127.0.0.1&lt;/code&gt; outside docstrings turns up exactly two matches, both &lt;code&gt;os.environ.get(…, &quot;mosquitto&quot;)&lt;/code&gt;-style defaults in &lt;code&gt;config.py&lt;/code&gt;. If step 9 (&#39;just change two env vars&#39;) had been false — a broker address buried in agent.py — that audit is what would have caught it before the guide shipped a wrong claim. Every AWS service named is real, current and non-deprecated; the IoT policy variable is a documented feature, not a strained analogy.&lt;/div&gt;" style="shape=note;size=16;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#F1F8E9;strokeColor=#2E7D32;strokeWidth=2;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=8;spacingTop=4;spacingRight=6;shadow=0;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="890" width="1640" height="140" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l58" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;line-height:1.5;font-size:10px;color:#90A4AE&quot;&gt;docs/11-aws-migration.md · 10-step ordered runbook: VPC &amp;#8594; data stores &amp;#8594; IoT Core &amp;#8594; ECR &amp;#8594; ECS &amp;#8594; ALB &amp;#8594; S3/CloudFront &amp;#8594; coturn &amp;#8594; robot config &amp;#8594; cut over&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=0;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1034" width="1640" height="24" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
